We have the system of equations:

\begin{align*}
x + y &= 153 \\
\frac{x}{y} &= 0.7 \\
\end{align*}

From the second equation, multiplying both sides by $y$ gives $x=.7y$. Next, substituting the second equation into the first to eliminate $x$ gives $.7y+y=153$, or $y=90$. Plugging in this value into the first equation in the original system of equations gives $x+90=153$ or $x=63$. Thus, $y-x=90-63=\boxed{27}$.

Since these equations look symmetric, at least on the left-hand side, we add all three together.  This gives us  \begin{align*}
&a(x+1+y)+b(y+x+1)+c(1+y+x)\\
&\qquad\qquad=x+7+2x+6y+4x+y.
\end{align*} We then factor out $(1+x+y)$ on the left and simplify the right side to get \begin{align*}
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7x+7y+7, \text{ so}\\
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7(1+x+y).
\end{align*}

Since $x+y\neq -1$, we know $x+y+1\neq 0$, so we can divide both sides by that term and find that $a+b+c=\boxed{7}$.

Cancel some factors of 2 before subtracting: \begin{align*}
\frac{2^{10}-2^8}{2^7-2^6}&=\frac{2^8(2^{2}-1)}{2^6(2^1-1)} \\
&=2^2\left(\frac{3}{1}\right) \\
&=\boxed{12}.
